    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A church claim is hard for a simple reason: the building is unusual and the people handling the claim are usually volunteers. Steeples, stained glass, vaulted sanctuaries, and organs require specialist repair pricing that a standard estimate won't include. And the trustee or facilities volunteer dealing with the carrier often has a full-time job elsewhere. We handle the claim, report to the board or committee you designate, and make sure the unusual parts of the building are priced by people who repair them.</p>

<h2>Steeples, bell towers, and tall walls</h2>
<p>Wind and hail hit the tallest parts of a church hardest, and those are the parts nobody can see well from the parking lot. Steeples and cupolas may be wood-framed and clad in metal, shingles, or fiberglass. Gable walls in a sanctuary can rise well above a typical commercial wall. Damage to flashings, louvers, cladding, and the cross or finial at the top is easy to miss from the ground.</p>
<p>Reaching these areas often requires a lift, crane, or specialty access, and that equipment cost belongs in the estimate along with the repair itself. We use drone photos to document areas that can't be reached safely, and we recommend a specialty contractor's input on steeple repairs, since general roofers often don't do that work.</p>

<h2>Stained glass and decorative windows</h2>
<p>Stained and leaded glass is one of the most contested items on a church claim. Questions that come up:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repair or replace.</strong> A cracked panel can often be releaded and repaired by a studio, but the whole window may need to be removed and shipped to do it.</li>
<li><strong>Protective glazing.</strong> Many stained glass windows have an exterior protective layer. Hail may break that layer without touching the art glass, and the protective layer still needs replacing.</li>
<li><strong>Valuation.</strong> Some windows are historic or irreplaceable. How they're valued depends on the policy: replacement cost, actual cash value, or a scheduled amount. Some policies limit glass or fine arts coverage unless the items are scheduled.</li>
</ul>
<p>We recommend a written assessment and quote from a stained glass studio, plus photos of every window from inside and out, before any window is removed.</p>

<h2>Sanctuaries with high ceilings</h2>
<p>Water from a roof leak in a sanctuary can travel along beams and decking before it shows up, and stains on a 30-foot ceiling are hard to inspect without scaffolding. Interior repairs in a sanctuary also tend to involve acoustic panels, decorative plaster or wood, specialty lighting, and pews or fixed seating that must be protected or moved. The cost of scaffolding and access is a real part of the repair. Insurer estimates written from floor level often leave it out or underprice it.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Organs, audio, and media equipment</h2>
<p>Pipe organs are sensitive to water, humidity, and temperature swings. After a water loss or a long power outage, an organ may need cleaning, regulation, or major repair by an organ builder. That is specialist work, and the insurer's estimate should rely on an organ technician's assessment, not a general contents allowance. Sound boards, speakers, projectors, streaming cameras, and pianos are usually claimed as business personal property, so check your contents limit and whether any items are scheduled. Keep damaged equipment until it has been inspected.</p>

<h2>Volunteer-run facilities and record-keeping</h2>
<p>Many congregations don't have a full-time facilities manager, so basic claim records end up scattered. Before or right after the first inspection, gather:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>The full policy, including declarations and any schedule of buildings and items</li>
<li>Photos of the property before the loss, often found in event and directory photos</li>
<li>Past roof or maintenance invoices that show the condition before the storm</li>
<li>Names of the people authorized to speak for the church and sign documents</li>
<li>Equipment lists or purchase records for audio, media, and musical instruments</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/tools/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help organize this. We give the board or committee a clear summary they can review at their next meeting, so decisions don't depend on one person relaying phone calls.</p>

<h2>Fellowship halls, schools, and daycares</h2>
<p>Most church campuses include more than a sanctuary: fellowship halls with commercial kitchens, education wings, gyms, offices, parsonages, and sometimes a daycare or school. Each building needs its own scope. Some of these uses may also produce income, such as daycare tuition or facility rental, and that income may be claimable if the policy includes business income coverage. A parsonage may be covered under the church policy or under a separate dwelling policy. We check how each building is listed so nothing falls between policies.</p>
<p>Hail on a sanctuary roof is handled like any <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail damage claim</a>, with the access costs added. When a congregation has already received a denial or a low offer, the <a href="/services/denied-underpaid-claims/">denied and underpaid claims</a> page walks through appraisal and other options. A church policy with an old building value may also face a coinsurance penalty, explained in our <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial claims overview</a>.</p>
